00:36:57.080 --> 00:37:05.610
Mark Blackman: So this proposal is to move two repos from Hashgraph to Hiero.

233
00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:11.659
Mark Blackman: These two repos are called Solo Provisioner and Solo Operator.

234
00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:19.370
Mark Blackman: And what these two… Pieces of software do is they manage the node lifecycle.

235
00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:25.299
Mark Blackman: Currently… Provisioner is used, and…

236
00:37:25.790 --> 00:37:29.700
Mark Blackman: softly mandated by the BlockNode team.

237
00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:40.529
Mark Blackman: So, the block node installation is fully automated from Provisioner, and that is really the motivation to move these repos

238
00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:49.609
Mark Blackman: into Hiero, because a Hiero node is already using it. But the future is to have these two tools manage

239
00:37:50.160 --> 00:37:53.879
Mark Blackman: Lifecycle operations for also the consensus node.

240
00:37:54.030 --> 00:38:07.150
Mark Blackman: And we have two hips in draft that we will be presenting soon to make those changes on the consensus node side, to use these two tools.

241
00:38:07.290 --> 00:38:14.100
Mark Blackman: Why is this important? Particularly on the consensus node, are…

242
00:38:14.340 --> 00:38:19.679
Mark Blackman: Our algorithm, our consensus algorithm, is very energy efficient.

243
00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:23.370
Mark Blackman: And… and just efficient in general.

244
00:38:24.650 --> 00:38:28.130
Mark Blackman: Because, all of…

245
00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:37.189
Mark Blackman: the software is deterministic, and so this cuts down on a lot of gossip if… if the nodes themselves come up

246
00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:40.100
Mark Blackman: with the Hashgraph independently.

247
00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:50.069
Mark Blackman: So, as a result of this, it brings a burden to node operators around the network, to ensure…

248
00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:55.549
Mark Blackman: That all the consensus nodes are running on the same software.

249
00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:57.600
Mark Blackman: At the same time.

250
00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:06.620
Mark Blackman: And right now, Hiero Hedera, Has network-based orchestration to facilitate this.

251
00:39:06.960 --> 00:39:14.000
Mark Blackman: So we actually have transactions that are sent to the network that instruct the nodes to upgrade

252
00:39:14.500 --> 00:39:20.270
Mark Blackman: Do a certain version at a certain time within consensus.

253
00:39:21.070 --> 00:39:29.299
Mark Blackman: This has… worked great, but we want to build onto this by using Kubernetes, technology.

254
00:39:29.590 --> 00:39:32.920
Mark Blackman: What this will allow us to do

255
00:39:33.090 --> 00:39:41.800
Mark Blackman: Most importantly, as ads… as nodes are added to the network, or their IP addresses change.

256
00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:45.229
Mark Blackman: This software allows those

257
00:39:45.380 --> 00:39:59.259
Mark Blackman: firewall rules to be automatically updated through the tool at the same time, right when those nodes are being added or deleted. So, the automatic firewall rule management

258
00:39:59.820 --> 00:40:11.619
Mark Blackman: Is hugely advantageous, particularly, again, as we have to orchestrate all the nodes on the network to be on the same software at the same time.

259
00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:19.949
Mark Blackman: So, there's really, 3 major parts of this solution.

260
00:40:20.480 --> 00:40:23.650
Mark Blackman: The first is what we call the shim.

261
00:40:23.890 --> 00:40:27.949
Mark Blackman: And this shim is what listens to the network.

262
00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:32.000
Mark Blackman: And communicates to provision or operator.

263
00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:38.879
Mark Blackman: So again, we have these transactions, freeze prepare, freeze upgrade.

264
00:40:39.050 --> 00:40:47.520
Mark Blackman: And the shim is what is listening to the network and sees, oh, okay, there's an upgrade coming. I need to prepare.

265
00:40:47.770 --> 00:40:51.139
Mark Blackman: Operator and provisioner for this.

266
00:40:52.320 --> 00:41:03.669
Mark Blackman: So we have the shim talking to the network, informing, these two pieces of software what to do. Provisioner works at the host level.

267
00:41:03.870 --> 00:41:06.639
Mark Blackman: So, provisioner is what is actually…

268
00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:17.000
Mark Blackman: upgrading the block node, eventually upgrading the consensus node from the host level. This is the software that is

269
00:41:17.570 --> 00:41:22.130
Mark Blackman: Actually, updating those firewall rules based on the address book.

270
00:41:22.310 --> 00:41:24.959
Mark Blackman: That are also discovered from the shim.

271
00:41:25.330 --> 00:41:32.730
Mark Blackman: And then the second piece is operator. Now, this is… Working at the cluster level.

272
00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:49.720
Mark Blackman: So, the shim, well, A, it's monitoring the state. If there is something wrong with the node, it will restart it. A lot of this cluster management, just as is

273
00:41:50.050 --> 00:41:53.609
Mark Blackman: is present in other Kubernetes solutions.

274
00:41:54.310 --> 00:42:03.440
Mark Blackman: So… These, again, provisioner is used by Blocknode. We're actively working to propose a HIP.

275
00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:07.240
Mark Blackman: Where the consensus nodes will also use this.

276
00:42:07.390 --> 00:42:22.260
Mark Blackman: This is really used by node operators and both block node and consensus node operators, and the benefit is it just removes any manual intervention required

277
00:42:22.450 --> 00:42:36.379
Mark Blackman: to accommodate, ensuring all nodes on the network are on the same software. So, node operators could, really leverage this. Obviously, platform engineers, SRE, release engineers, also.

